        "Walk through any intensive care unit and you will hear it: a constant chorus of beeps, chimes and buzzes. Monitors sound when a patient’s heart rate rises, when oxygen levels fall, when a drip runs low or when a sensor slips off a finger. Each alarm was designed with a good purpose — to make sure that no sign of danger goes unnoticed. Yet together they have created a problem that hospitals around the world now take seriously. Nurses call it “alarm fatigue.”",
        "The difficulty is that the great majority of alarms do not signal real emergencies. In one hospital study, researchers reviewed every alarm produced in a children’s ward over several weeks and found that fewer than one in a hundred required staff to change a patient’s treatment. Many were triggered by a child moving in bed or by settings that were too sensitive for that particular patient. {u:(1):When staff hear hundreds of such signals in a single shift, they gradually learn, without meaning to, that an alarm usually means nothing.} Responses slow down, alarms are silenced without a careful check, and on rare occasions a true warning is missed.",
        "{u:(2):The cost of constant noise is not limited to the staff.} Patients in intensive care already sleep poorly, and repeated alarms during the night make things worse. Lack of sleep has been linked to confusion in elderly patients, slower recovery and a weaker immune response. Families sitting by the bedside also report that the sounds make them anxious, even when nothing is wrong. In addition, some nurses describe feeling constantly on edge, unable to concentrate on one task before another sound calls them away.",
        "How can hospitals reduce the noise without reducing safety? One approach is to adjust alarm limits to suit each patient instead of using the same factory settings for everyone. A healthy athlete recovering from surgery, for example, may have a resting heart rate low enough to set off a standard alarm again and again. Another is to add a short delay, so that a reading must stay outside the safe range for several seconds before the alarm sounds; brief changes caused by movement then disappear on their own. Simple habits help too, such as changing sensor pads every day so that they stay firmly attached.",
        "Hospitals that have tried such measures report striking results. One adult unit reduced the number of alarms per bed by almost half within three months, and staff said they now responded to alarms more quickly than before. {u:(3):Some engineers argue that the real solution lies in smarter machines that combine information from several sensors and judge whether a pattern is truly dangerous.} Such systems are being tested, but experts warn that {u:(4):a machine that decides on its own when to stay silent raises difficult questions about who is responsible if something goes wrong.}",
